COPY zone3.chat_messages (id, session_id, role, content, cited_chunk_ids, abstained, created_at, llm_model, latency_ms, chunk_count, embedding) FROM stdin;
c095658f-4600-44f8-a1d2-57597ddd1a6f	9e69ad9a-be07-4f6a-8690-0da3936389c9	user	Problem in webhook latency?	\N	f	2026-09-08 12:50:21.047641+00	\N	\N	\N	\N
1809a1a6-9a9b-4ce2-9cef-6091163f4a90	9e69ad9a-be07-4f6a-8690-0da3936389c9	assistant	The webhook latency problem shows up when more than about fifty issues arrive in a minute, at which point the handler begins dropping events and callers experience a 30‑second timeout instead of a normal response [KPD-33] [KPD-48]. The symptom was first seen during a business‑hour deploy while hitting services such as the secrets scanner and the retrieval service, and the logs pointed to `security/provenance.py` returning inconsistent results under load, suggesting a race or stale‑cache condition [KPD-19] [KPD-48]. The issue was traced to recent changes in the authentication middleware and the `ingestion/linkage.py` component that were shipped in v0.17, which appear to have triggered the handler’s inability to keep up with the burst traffic [KPD-135] [KPD-19]. A commit explicitly notes that the Jira webhook handler drops events during bursts of over 50 issues per minute, confirming the root cause of the latency spikes [258a4a92ad1c4492ca413dfab879b5aa49538e32] [KPD-30]. No fix is recorded in the supplied tickets.	{a994f778-113e-432f-8718-5a9d7171687d,1e9c9631-e59a-49e9-9440-cca84e66b5b6,beb4b679-9cfc-49fe-b6e4-9431d890d508,10e0b44d-8f7b-4f8e-9033-b9ac756e626b,9c73fb16-f458-4b39-905b-ee083875130c}	f	2026-09-08 12:50:23.024775+00	\N	\N	\N	\N
83ab67bb-fedf-4c3f-a9dc-4059e23e2486	9e69ad9a-be07-4f6a-8690-0da3936389c9	user	What was the Embedding Retrival OOms?	\N	f	2026-09-11 11:53:45.400666+00	\N	\N	\N	\N
92c3a1ea-28d7-43d3-9b51-efe2e7fd6c4d	9e69ad9a-be07-4f6a-8690-0da3936389c9	assistant	The out‑of‑memory (OOM) condition happened during the continuous‑integration run of the embedding‑batch test. It was triggered because the test’s corpus fixture had grown large enough to exhaust the CI runner’s memory limits. The failure was recorded in a commit that notes the CI runner OOMs on the embedding batch test due to the enlarged corpus fixture [18fa172d70c525fffb309800ffb0679c796335dc]. No other details about the OOM are provided in the available tickets or commits.	{c97492ab-fccc-4c2c-98c5-49ddb2039234}	f	2026-09-11 11:53:47.393864+00	\N	\N	\N	\N
dec61f7e-441c-4d60-8bbf-0d36f628d84e	9e69ad9a-be07-4f6a-8690-0da3936389c9	user	Problem with chunk overlapping?	\N	f	2026-09-11 11:54:45.480636+00	\N	\N	\N	\N
992ac733-521b-48c8-af97-8a9364876c27	9e69ad9a-be07-4f6a-8690-0da3936389c9	assistant	The issue was that the configured chunk‑overlap value was being ignored whenever a Markdown file contained nested code blocks, so the generated chunks did not include the intended overlapping context and could break downstream processing [ a054d937b4205d07a4a8c8ef0f8d03dd235aed06 ]. Several commits were added to fix this by explicitly handling nested code blocks and ensuring the overlap setting is applied during chunking of Markdown content [1325c002add6d5cc1700a06c4bc4d5315fdaed7d][801c81a8b3f5c8f74ef177eb504adcb702e6d3fc][f721d44ed757f380e543f8053f17fd981d7b850f][f8a7c26a6efc74c8c17bf489921b5525310a628a]. After the change, Markdown files with nested code blocks respect the overlap configuration, restoring correct chunk boundaries.	{d3860d92-1b5e-4aea-a6be-2c82ee8d0227,102d89d8-948e-43f2-aaab-2cec89bc5352,d4a6a65d-9741-47d7-95f1-c8046bed725a,4aa536f6-a4df-4f9e-8ea9-c6279f317b18,e3098283-e553-4537-9464-a936ce73137f}	f	2026-09-11 11:54:47.157464+00	\N	\N	\N	\N
4f2b2feb-303b-4f0d-932f-862759143b80	9e69ad9a-be07-4f6a-8690-0da3936389c9	user	Deploy pipeline doesn't roll back automatically on a failed health check	\N	f	2026-09-11 11:58:10.053607+00	\N	\N	\N	\N
1786e09a-5131-4c31-afdb-25c26930274a	9e69ad9a-be07-4f6a-8690-0da3936389c9	assistant	The deploy pipeline was supposed to automatically revert a release when the post‑deploy health check fails, but after v0.14 it stopped doing so, leading to requests timing‑out (30 s) during a reindex and to the retrieval service returning inconsistent results under load [KPD-215]. Investigation linked the regression to a recent change in ingestion/jira_client.py and raised the possibility of a race condition or stale‑cache problem in retrieval/hybrid.py that prevented the health check from detecting the failure [KPD-216]. The issue was addressed in the merge that brought in the “feat/kpd‑195‑deploy‑pipeline‑doesnt‑roll‑back‑63” changes, which restored the automatic rollback behavior on health‑check failure [810fbcba16fca29076eb9d80b10a5b315318a486].	{f229eaa4-443a-43c7-a111-eb00f041fb19,4e3cee14-e7e7-4be9-b2de-ac80d405e47e,5ad3ea1b-a6d4-4a8b-9b8f-2357894e420b}	f	2026-09-11 11:58:11.962653+00	\N	\N	\N	\N
