a63a1f78-478d-4a01-80b7-9791918b6fff	cb2c5812-ab15-4220-92a2-c9bdaa919e33	user	Why did the ask endpoint return 500 instead of an error message?	\N	f	2026-09-13 18:05:41.015562+00	\N	\N	\N	\N
74fe87a8-189b-44b6-a139-cce1d8c3c2dc	cb2c5812-ab15-4220-92a2-c9bdaa919e33	assistant	The ask endpoint was hitting a downstream rate‑limit and the code that should have caught that condition and returned a proper 429 response instead let the exception propagate, so the HTTP layer fell back to a generic 500 error with no stack trace. In the retrieval service the lack of graceful degradation caused the 500 (as reported in KPD‑90) and in the scope‑classifier a stale‑cache hit produced the same symptom (KPD‑107). The underlying cause was traced to inconsistent behaviour in security/provenance.py under load, which appears to be a race or stale‑cache issue that prevented the proper error handling from being executed (KPD‑88). All of these tickets point to the same root problem: the downstream rate‑limit was not being translated into a 429 because the error‑handling path was broken, resulting in a 500 response.	{}	f	2026-09-13 18:05:42.511214+00	\N	\N	\N	\N
