e12fb6e0-b697-494d-b80f-e932afa5449d	750eed9a-c45d-4431-a69d-942cf4aad74a	assistant	Overall, the open tickets assigned to Agrim concentrate on reliability, error‑handling, and security gaps in CI pipelines, deployment automation, and webhook integrations.\n\n- KPD-225: GitHub rate limit handling ignores the secondary limit on the PR review endpoint; this can produce inconsistent API results under load, jeopardizing integration stability. [KPD-225]  \n- KPD-216: Deploy pipeline does not roll back automatically after a failed health check; without rollback, a bad deployment can leave services in an unstable state. [KPD-216]  \n- KPD-211: CI pipeline flakes on the eval stage (~1 in 8 runs) with a ConnectionPoolExhausted error from the handover brief endpoint; flakiness undermines test confidence and slows releases. [KPD-211]  \n- KPD-205: CI runner OOMs on the embedding batch test as the corpus fixture grew; out‑of‑memory crashes halt CI execution and delay development. [KPD-205]  \n- KPD-202: Auth provider webhook signature verification is skipped in one code path, causing 500 errors with no stack trace; this weakens security checks and hampers troubleshooting. [KPD-202]  \n- KPD-201: Auth provider webhook signature verification is also skipped during a schema migration, returning a stale cache hit instead of proper authentication; this masks auth failures and can lead to data inconsistency. [KPD-201]  \n- KPD-178: Canary token trigger does not fire an alert when the webhook endpoint is unreachable; missing alerts reduce visibility into downstream service outages. [KPD-178]	{d3eb3c08-c059-4f3d-b8ab-acbcef31dbff,f229eaa4-443a-43c7-a111-eb00f041fb19,dcb3743f-f8c8-4e3f-825a-e3dc855ffc7d,ff247cac-738c-49f5-a13b-4e6bcf02a5e6,3ee64df6-b1ff-4443-a37a-15bd09949f98,6366d7a0-a7e2-4f44-a195-637f4edeb9fa,16b3f429-08f3-4c1a-ba63-443c58aad786}	f	2026-09-14 15:00:42.67922+00	groq	4288	7	\N
