b00cfe67-547a-4c19-912d-9292aa5d4c46	shubhr@relay.dev	acme-data-migration-20d649	Auto-draft: KPD-36	The issue stemmed from the event listener that reacts to Jira issue label updates; it only fired when the issue’s status changed, so pure label modifications never triggered the downstream linkage recomputation. The fix rewrites the listener to subscribe directly to label‑change events and forces a recompute whenever the label set differs from the cached version, ensuring the linkage graph stays in sync. When extending this logic, be careful not to re‑introduce the same shortcut by filtering out label‑only changes, and verify that any new bulk‑update paths also emit the appropriate events.	approved	\N	2026-09-14 09:45:37.807438+00	2026-09-14 09:45:37.803627+00	\N	1	2026-09-14 09:45:37.803627+00	\N	KPD-36
6c28ac91-9a1a-4d5c-8480-1c695057546c	akshar@relay.dev	acme-data-migration-20d649	Auto-draft: KPD-109	The issue stemmed from the scope endpoint persisting the classification result in an in‑memory cache and never invalidating it when a Statement of Work (SOW) document was re‑uploaded, causing stale data to be returned. The fix clears or refreshes the cached entry as part of the upload handling, ensuring the endpoint recomputes the classification based on the new document. Future developers should be mindful that any other operations that modify the underlying SOW data must also trigger cache invalidation, and they should verify that the cache key includes versioning or timestamps to avoid similar stale‑cache bugs.	draft	\N	\N	2026-09-14 09:45:30.297665+00	\N	1	2026-09-14 09:45:30.297665+00	{b96c4b64-14c9-4fb2-abe5-4fa6c42bf4c1,36143605-b93a-46db-a944-45613fd287d1}	KPD-109
ebabbfbd-503e-4282-b550-39b9418933ef	akshar@relay.dev	acme-data-migration-20d649	Auto-draft: KPD-104	The bug stemmed from the pagination cursor assuming a static ordering of results; when the user changed the sort order while still on the same page, the cursor pointed to items that no longer matched the new sort, causing gaps or duplicates. The fix normalizes the cursor by tying it to a deterministic sort key and resetting it whenever the sort criteria change, ensuring the next page always starts from the correct position. Future developers should remember that any change to the sort parameters must invalidate or recompute the cursor, and that the cursor logic should remain decoupled from mutable query state to avoid similar regressions.	approved	\N	2026-09-14 09:45:31.127367+00	2026-09-14 09:45:31.12381+00	\N	1	2026-09-14 09:45:31.12381+00	\N	KPD-104
