48d1a878-c305-4fd2-a156-6eed1277c9ac	jason@relay.dev	acme-data-migration-20d649	Auto-draft: KPD-13	The ingestion pipeline was previously slicing comment results into multiple pages when a body exceeded 32 KB, which caused downstream services to miss or duplicate data during aggregation. The new implementation caps the stored body at 32 KB at ingest time, eliminating the need for pagination and ensuring a single, deterministic record per comment. This approach works because the storage schema already enforces a 32 KB limit, so truncating early aligns with the persisted format and avoids extra paging logic. Future changes that adjust the size limit or introduce variable‑length fields should revisit this truncation step to prevent silent data loss or re‑introducing pagination.	draft	\N	\N	2026-09-14 09:45:31.943257+00	\N	1	2026-09-14 09:45:31.943257+00	{b6f2c53c-4bdf-40ef-b5aa-b10ecf809d3e,80430367-daa9-479a-9132-f896d992d622}	KPD-13
d6960601-964c-4d6a-a3bf-f50bdae268b2	jason@relay.dev	acme-data-migration-20d649	Auto-draft: KPD-186	The issue was that during a deployment the authentication service could issue a new token while existing requests were still using the old one, causing those in‑flight calls to fail with unauthorized errors. The fix introduces a synchronized refresh flow that queues any outgoing requests until the token renewal completes, ensuring all requests are sent with a valid token and eliminating the race condition. This approach works because it centralizes token handling and guarantees that only one refresh operation can be active at a time. Future developers should be careful not to re‑introduce parallel refresh paths or bypass the queue when adding new auth‑related interceptors, as that would resurrect the race.	approved	\N	2026-09-14 09:45:32.696526+00	2026-09-14 09:45:32.692919+00	\N	1	2026-09-14 09:45:32.692919+00	\N	KPD-186
392ded59-8443-4ba1-a420-1e7aaf7e264f	omar@relay.dev	acme-data-migration-20d649	Auto-draft: KPD-113	The sprint planning document still pointed to an epic that had been divided into two separate epics after the scope change, causing confusion and broken links for the team’s tracking tools. The update replaces the stale reference with the correct identifiers for the new epics, restoring accurate navigation and ensuring that future sprint reports pull the right data. When epics are re-scoped or split, double‑check any static documentation or scripts that embed epic IDs, as they won’t update automatically. This will prevent similar mismatches and keep the planning artifacts in sync with Jira.	draft	\N	\N	2026-09-14 09:45:33.489691+00	\N	1	2026-09-14 09:45:33.489691+00	{c1a5df61-f438-4d64-83cd-6d8f2e2e2e8d,5adcb883-0f58-4613-a3bd-f24a67c95a40}	KPD-113
4bb17b43-289b-499c-b9bd-da6c292816eb	shubhr@relay.dev	acme-data-migration-20d649	Scratchpad eval loop	Auto-draft evaluation runs as its own asyncio task (_scratchpad_eval_loop), deliberately not inside _sync_loop — an LLM call there must never slow down the Jira/GitHub poll.	approved	\N	2026-09-14 10:04:42.733376+00	2026-09-14 10:04:42.733376+00	\N	1	2026-09-14 10:04:42.733376+00	\N	\N
17b4bfb8-3f50-4f8d-b9a4-0777461dbc3c	shubhr@relay.dev	acme-data-migration-20d649	is_note_worthy	The note-worthy filter is recall-first: any one rule matching (commit count, issue type, priority, keyword) qualifies. Better an extra draft than a missed fix.	approved	\N	2026-09-14 10:04:42.734409+00	2026-09-14 10:04:42.734409+00	\N	1	2026-09-14 10:04:42.734409+00	\N	\N
5041e8c0-9f97-4859-9a34-e494ad963fc9	omar@relay.dev	acme-data-migration-20d649	Auto-draft: KPD-95	The issue was caused by the request handler reading the entire PDF payload into memory before validating its size, which caused the Node process to run out of heap space when PDFs with embedded images exceeded ~5 MB, resulting in 500 errors. The fix adds a streaming size check that aborts the upload as soon as the byte limit is crossed, preventing the full file from being buffered and allowing the server to return a controlled 413 response instead. Future developers should be careful not to re‑introduce synchronous file reads or remove the streaming guard when refactoring the route, and ensure any downstream middleware respects the early termination of the request stream.	approved	\N	2026-09-14 09:45:34.372075+00	2026-09-14 09:45:34.369548+00	\N	1	2026-09-14 09:45:34.369548+00	\N	KPD-95
ff652f31-c8b9-4158-9c7b-fa68c1de8c94	priya@relay.dev	acme-data-migration-20d649	Auto-draft: KPD-99	The original implementation keyed the rate‑limiting logic to the API key, which allowed multiple users on the same corporate network to share a single limit and effectively bypass throttling. By switching the key to the client’s IP address, each distinct network endpoint now consumes its own quota, ensuring that shared‑office IPs are correctly penalized and overall traffic is throttled as intended. The new logic assumes that IP addresses are stable identifiers; if the service later moves behind a load balancer or starts using NAT‑ed client IPs, the limiter will need to be adjusted (e.g., by incorporating X‑Forwarded‑For handling or a more granular identifier). Also, be aware that aggressive IP‑based limiting can unintentionally affect legitimate users on dynamic IP pools, so monitor false‑positive rates after deployment.	draft	\N	\N	2026-09-14 09:45:35.168753+00	\N	1	2026-09-14 09:45:35.168753+00	{c17ad713-b0d7-40a6-9d69-76e457d902ae,06771a7a-5771-4e19-a0fc-f6851c7bcf66,fbdedffa-c429-41c1-a655-367e5318e506}	KPD-99
29945706-1e4a-4d04-9082-cae1d7f45aed	priya@relay.dev	acme-data-migration-20d649	Auto-draft: KPD-239	The issue was that permission checks were performed only at request time, so if a user’s project access was revoked after a token was issued the system still granted read access based on the stale token data. The fix moves the evaluation to a read‑time lookup that re‑queries the current project‑access table, ensuring the latest revocation state is respected. This approach is safe because it does not rely on cached permissions, but it does add an extra DB hit for every read operation, so future developers should monitor the query performance and consider caching strategies that invalidate on revocation events.	approved	\N	2026-09-14 09:45:36.015905+00	2026-09-14 09:45:36.012023+00	\N	1	2026-09-14 09:45:36.012023+00	\N	KPD-239
48633218-3d13-4f8d-a6ab-4a86e4978a5a	shubhr@relay.dev	acme-data-migration-20d649	Auto-draft: KPD-111	The issue stemmed from the PDF generation service attempting to embed images larger than 5 MB directly into SOW PDFs, which caused the `/api/routes/scope` endpoint to exceed memory limits and return 500 errors. The fix adds a pre‑validation step that checks the size of each image and, when the threshold is exceeded, skips embedding the image and instead inserts a placeholder reference, thereby keeping the PDF payload within safe bounds and preventing the server crash. Future developers should be aware that this size check only applies to images embedded via this endpoint, so any new code paths that handle PDF creation must also enforce the same limit or implement similar streaming/placeholder logic to avoid re‑introducing the memory overflow. Additionally, keep an eye on any downstream services that might rely on the presence of those images, as they will now receive PDFs with placeholders instead of the full assets.	draft	\N	\N	2026-09-14 09:45:36.991073+00	\N	1	2026-09-14 09:45:36.991073+00	{d7196f50-01f1-46d2-a3ae-608b203ce659,ea65f1be-b827-4084-a648-77da36220bac}	KPD-111
0d82b286-d642-4c1f-8af8-d8b0271b9fa8	agrim@relay.dev	acme-data-migration-20d649	Auto-draft: KPD-142	The issue stemmed from the read‑time permission check ignoring cases where a user’s project access had been revoked, causing stale permissions to be granted during data retrieval. The fix adds a validation step that re‑evaluates the user’s current project rights at the point of read, ensuring that any revocation is respected before the operation proceeds. This also required updating the ticket‑to‑commit parsing logic to correctly recognize references wrapped in parentheses, which previously broke the linkage. Future developers should verify that any new permission‑related code re‑queries the latest access state and that commit‑link regexes continue to handle both plain and parenthesized ticket IDs.	promoted	\N	2026-09-14 09:45:29.429918+00	2026-09-14 09:45:29.425294+00	\N	1	2026-09-14 09:45:29.425294+00	\N	KPD-142
1ac8e8ce-7bb3-4b7f-a628-aa13a75a3ad3	agrim@relay.dev	acme-data-migration-20d649	Env var gotcha	RELAY_ENGAGEMENT_ID must exactly match project_staffing.engagement_id — a stray suffix silently returns zero staffing rows, not an error.	approved	\N	2026-09-14 10:04:42.718335+00	2026-09-14 10:04:42.718335+00	\N	1	2026-09-14 10:04:42.718335+00	\N	\N
